The most practical scope problem is injecting a **request-scoped bean into a singleton**. A singleton is created once at startup — long before any HTTP request exists.

**Why it breaks**
- A singleton holds a direct reference to a request-scoped bean — that reference points to nothing (or a stale instance from a previous request)
- Spring tries to create the request-scoped bean at startup, outside any request, and throws `BeanCreationException` with the message `'no thread-bound request found'`

**The fix — scoped proxy**
- Declare `@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)`
- Spring injects a CGLIB proxy instead of the real bean
- The proxy delegates every method call to the current request's actual instance, looked up from `RequestAttributes` at call time

```java
@Component
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class RequestContext {
    private String correlationId;
}

@Service
public class AuditService {
    private final RequestContext ctx;
    public AuditService(RequestContext ctx) { this.ctx = ctx; }
}
```

**Proxy mode options**
- `ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS` uses CGLIB subclass proxy — works for concrete classes
- `ScopedProxyMode.INTERFACES` uses JDK dynamic proxy — only works if the bean implements an interface
- Most teams default to `TARGET_CLASS` for flexibility

Beyond the five built-in scopes, Spring supports **custom scopes** essential for tenant isolation in multi-tenant SaaS applications and batch processing contexts.

**Implementing a custom scope**
- Register a `Scope` implementation that manages object lifecycle within your custom boundary
- Typically uses `ThreadLocal` to store scoped instances per thread
- Register via `beanFactory.registerScope("tenant", new TenantScope())`

```java
public class TenantScope implements Scope {
    private final ThreadLocal<Map<String, Object>> scopedObjects =
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(HashMap::new);

    @Override
    public Object get(String name, ObjectFactory<?> factory) {
        Map<String, Object> scope = scopedObjects.get();
        return scope.computeIfAbsent(name, k -> factory.getObject());
    }

    @Override
    public Object remove(String name) {
        return scopedObjects.get().remove(name);
    }
}
```

**Scope proxy internals — the per-call cost**
- Every method call on a scoped proxy involves a scope lookup — roughly 50 nanoseconds overhead
- For hot paths, cache the result in a local variable rather than calling proxy methods repeatedly
- The CGLIB proxy is shared across all consumers — only the underlying instance changes per scope boundary

**`@RefreshScope` (Spring Cloud)**
- Beans annotated with `@RefreshScope` are destroyed and recreated when `/actuator/refresh` is called
- Enables dynamic configuration updates without restart
- Caveat: in-flight requests using the old bean instance continue with stale state until completion

Calling `reset()` doesn't make this safe. Two threads calling `generate()` concurrently both call `reset()` and `loadData()` on the same `context` object, interleaving their data.

```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ReportContext context;

    public ReportService(ReportContext context) {
        this.context = context;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        context.reset();
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

The `context` field was injected exactly once at singleton creation time. Every call to `generate()` operates on the same prototype instance — race conditions in single-process, state accumulation in single-threaded use.

`ObjectProvider` wraps the bean factory's prototype creation — functionally equivalent to `applicationContext.getBean(ReportContext.class)` but without coupling your code to the `ApplicationContext` API.

```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider;

    public ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider) {
        this.contextProvider = contextProvider;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        ReportContext context = contextProvider.getObject();
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Each call to `getObject()` creates a fresh `ReportContext`. No sharing, no reset, no thread-safety concerns. Spring does **not** manage prototype destruction — the instance returned from `getObject()` is yours to manage. If it holds resources (connections, file handles), close them explicitly; `@PreDestroy` is never called.

The first two phases happen in quick succession but are **distinct operations** with different timing characteristics.

**Instantiation (Phase 1)**
- Spring calls the constructor — either the no-arg constructor or the `@Autowired` constructor
- For constructor injection, dependencies are resolved from the context and passed as arguments
- Dependencies are available from the first line of the constructor body

**Dependency Injection (Phase 2)**
- After the constructor returns, `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` runs in the `postProcessProperties` phase
- Uses reflection to scan for `@Autowired`, `@Value`, and `@Inject` annotations on fields and setters
- Calls `Field.setAccessible(true)` and writes the resolved bean directly into the field
- If a required dependency cannot be resolved, the context fails to start

**The crucial timing difference**
- Field-injected dependencies are **null inside the constructor body** because the post-processor hasn't run yet
- Reading an `@Autowired` field in a constructor causes `NullPointerException`

```java
@Component
public class ReportService {
    @Autowired private DataSource dataSource;

    public ReportService() {
        // dataSource is NULL — injection has not happened yet
        // dataSource.getConnection(); -> NullPointerException
    }

    @PostConstruct
    void init() {
        dataSource.getConnection();
    }
}
```

**The fix**
- Use constructor injection — dependencies available from line one
- Or move initialization logic to `@PostConstruct` — fires after all injection is complete

When the `ApplicationContext` shuts down — through `context.close()` or the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring calls destruction callbacks on **singleton beans in reverse creation order**.

**Destruction callback order (mirrors initialization)**
- `@PreDestroy` first — JSR-250 standard, recommended
- `DisposableBean.destroy()` second — Spring's older interface approach
- `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")` last — for third-party classes

```java
@Component
public class ConnectionPool {
    private final HikariDataSource ds;

    @PreDestroy
    void shutdown() {
        ds.close();
        log.info("Pool closed gracefully");
    }
}
```

**Reverse order matters**
- If `OrderService` depends on `PaymentService`, `OrderService` is destroyed first
- Guarantees `PaymentService` is still functional during `OrderService` cleanup
- Avoids cascading null references during shutdown

**Two cases where `@PreDestroy` does NOT run**
- **Prototype beans** — Spring does not track prototype instances after creation; no reference to call destroy on
- **SIGKILL or `kill -9`** — the JVM dies without executing any shutdown hooks; only SIGTERM, `docker stop`, and Kubernetes graceful termination trigger `@PreDestroy`

**Production consequence**
- Never store critical state in `@PreDestroy` — persist it during normal operation
- Use `@PreDestroy` only for releasing local resources the kernel won't clean up: connection pools, thread pools, file locks

When a bean uses multiple initialization mechanisms, Spring fires them in a **specific order**. Understanding this order matters because it determines which callback sees which state.

**1. `@PostConstruct` fires first**
- JSR-250 standard annotation — works in any Jakarta EE container, not just Spring
- Dependencies are fully injected when it runs
- Recommended for all your own classes

**2. `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()` fires second**
- Spring's older interface-based approach
- Couples your code to the Spring API — not recommended for new classes
- Still appears in Spring's own framework code and legacy applications

**3. `@Bean(initMethod = "setup")` fires last**
- Specified as a string in the bean definition; called via reflection
- The approach for third-party classes you can't annotate

```java
@Component
public class CacheWarmer implements InitializingBean {
    @Autowired private ProductRepository repo;

    @PostConstruct
    void postConstruct() { log.info("1. @PostConstruct"); }

    @Override
    public void afterPropertiesSet() { log.info("2. afterPropertiesSet"); }

    void customInit() { log.info("3. initMethod"); }
}
```

**Practical rule**
- Use `@PostConstruct` for your own classes
- Use `@Bean(initMethod)` for third-party beans
- Avoid `InitializingBean` in new code

When `ApplicationContext.close()` is called — either explicitly or through the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring iterates through all singleton beans in **reverse creation order** and calls their destruction callbacks.

**Destruction sequence (mirrors initialization)**
- `@PreDestroy` first
- `DisposableBean.destroy()` second
- `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")` last

**Reverse order ensures dependency safety**
- A bean is destroyed before the beans it depends on
- `OrderService` (depends on `PaymentService`) destroyed first; `PaymentService` still functional during cleanup

```java
@Component
public class MetricsReporter {
    private ScheduledExecutorService scheduler;

    @PostConstruct
    void startReporting() {
        scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::report, 0, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    @PreDestroy
    void stopReporting() throws InterruptedException {
        scheduler.shutdown();
        scheduler.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

**The prototype trap**
- Spring creates prototypes on demand but never tracks them — `@PreDestroy` is never called
- Switching a singleton to prototype scope without manual cleanup leaks resources silently

**The auto-detected destroyMethod**
- Beans registered via `@Bean` have implicit `destroyMethod` inferred by looking for `close()` or `shutdown()` methods
- Set `@Bean(destroyMethod = "")` to disable this if auto-detection causes problems

When you annotate a bean with `@Lazy`, Spring doesn't skip the bean definition — it still registers the bean in the container. What changes is the **instantiation timing**.

**The mechanism**
- Instead of creating the bean at startup, Spring injects a **CGLIB proxy** wherever the bean is needed
- The proxy holds a reference to the `BeanFactory` and the target bean's name
- The first method call on the proxy triggers `BeanFactory.getBean()`, which creates the real bean and caches it
- All subsequent calls go to the cached instance — no per-call overhead after the first

```java
@Service
@Lazy
public class ReportEngine {
    public ReportEngine() {
        loadHeavyResources();
    }
}

@Service
public class DashboardService {
    private final ReportEngine engine;

    public DashboardService(@Lazy ReportEngine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }

    public Report generate() {
        return engine.build();
    }
}
```

**Two placement strategies**
- `@Lazy` on the bean class — affects all injection points
- `@Lazy` on a specific injection-point parameter — affects only that consumer; one service might need the bean eagerly while another tolerates lazy initialization
- Parameter-level placement is more common — it gives fine-grained control

**Fix 1: Extract the shared concern**
- If A needs B and B needs A, they likely share logic that belongs in a third class C
- Both A and B depend on C instead of each other
- Eliminates the cycle entirely and often improves cohesion

**Fix 2: Replace one direction with events**
- If A needs to notify B but B also needs to query A, replace the notification with a Spring event
- A publishes, B listens — B can still depend on A for queries (one-directional)
- A no longer depends on B

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    public void processPayment(Payment p) {
        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCompleted(p.getId()));
    }
}

@Component
public class NotificationListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onPayment(PaymentCompleted event) {
        // notification logic — no dependency on PaymentService
    }
}
```

**Fix 3: Interface segregation**
- If B only needs one method from A, extract that method into a narrow interface that A implements
- B depends on the interface, not on A directly
- Often reveals that B didn't really need 'all of A' — it needed a specific capability

**Why `@Lazy` is the wrong answer**
- It masks the cycle by deferring creation — the code works, but the design problem remains
- When someone removes the `@Lazy` annotation during refactoring (not knowing it was load-bearing), `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException` returns
- Use only as a temporary workaround with a `// TODO: fix circular dependency` comment

**Why `spring.main.allow-circular-references=true` is the wrong answer**
- Restores legacy silent resolution — partially-constructed beans get injected
- Subtle runtime bugs replace startup failures
- The Spring community changed the default for a reason
